rce="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;A meta-learning approach for&#10;selectivity prediction in&#10;asymmetric catalysis&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4409453348&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;Data-Efficient, Chemistry-Aware&#10;Machine Learning Predictions of&#10;Diels–Alder Reaction Outcomes&#10;year: 2024&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4392515737&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 6727&#10;trend_chars: 9890" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="968" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: Will AI redefine the&#10;future of chemistry? Context: AI and machine learning have the&#10;potential to greatly benefit organic chemistry, leading to&#10;more rapid synthesis of molecules. These computational&#10;technologies can greatly optimize and accelerate drug&#10;development by, for example, predicting successful pathways to&#10;synthesize target molecules. seed_paper: MechRetro is a&#10;chemical-mechanism-driven graph learning framework for&#10;interpretable retrosynthesis prediction and pathway planning #&#10;CITATION SUBGRAPH (3 refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1]&#10;Permutation invariant graph-to-sequence model for template-&#10;free retrosynthesis and reaction prediction (2021, venue=N/A)&#10;authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1 cited_in_sections = []&#10;cite_count = 4 section_weight = 0.0 delta_year = unknown&#10;is_influential = False low_confidence = True cited_by_subgraph&#10;= 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[] delta_yr=unknown&#10;edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: Synthesis planning and reaction&#10;outcome prediction are two fundamental problems in computer-&#10;aided organic chemistry for which a variety of data-driven&#10;approaches have emerged. Natural language approaches that&#10;model each problem as a SMILES-to-SMILES translation lead to a&#10;simple end-to-end formulatio Existing Methods: Unavailable&#10;from local metadata. Motivation: Synthesis planning and&#10;reaction outcome prediction are two fundamental problems in&#10;computer-aided organic chemistry for which a variety of data-&#10;driven approaches have emerged. Natural language approaches&#10;that model each problem as a SMILES-to-SMILES translation lead&#10;to a simple end-to-end formulatio Proposed Method: Synthesis&#10;planning and reaction outcome prediction are two fundamental&#10;problems in computer-aided organic chemistry for which a&#10;variety of data-driven approaches have emerged. Natural&#10;language approaches that model each problem as a SMILES-to-&#10;SMILES translation lead to a simple end-to-end formulatio&#10;Experiment Plan: Unavailable from local metadata. [ABSTRACT]&#10;Synthesis planning and reaction outcome prediction are two&#10;fundamental problems in computer-aided organic chemistry for&#10;which a variety of data-driven approaches have emerged.&#10;Natural language approaches that model each problem as a&#10;SMILES-to-SMILES translation lead to a simple end-to-end&#10;formulation, reduce the nee... ## [2] A meta-learning approach&#10;for selectivity prediction in asymmetric catalysis (2025,&#10;venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1&#10;cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 9 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = False low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: Transition&#10;metal-catalyzed asymmetric reactions are of high contemporary&#10;importance in organic synthesis. Recently, machine learning&#10;(ML) has shown promise in accelerating the development of&#10;newer catalytic protocols. However, the need for large amount&#10;of experimental data can present a bottleneck f Existing&#10;Methods: Unavailable from local metadata. Motivation:&#10;Transition metal-catalyzed asymmetric reactions are of high&#10;contemporary importance in organic synthesis. Recently,&#10;machine learning (ML) h...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: *(For chronological coherence, I treat the 2021 graph-&#10;to-sequence paper as Paper 0, the 2024 Diels–Alder/NERF paper&#10;as Paper 1, and the 2025 meta-learning paper as Paper 2. The&#10;supplied subgraph is a shallow citation neighborhood, so the&#10;progression is inferred from the evolution of problem framing&#10;rather than from long explicit predecessor chains.)* Paper 0&#10;to Paper 1: The 2021 work, **“Permutation invariant graph-to-&#10;sequence model for template-free retrosynthesis and reaction&#10;prediction,”** represents the phase in which AI for chemistry&#10;began moving away from purely text-based SMILES-to-SMILES&#10;translation and template-dependent rules toward **graph-based,&#10;end-to-end molecular reasoning**. Its core contribution is to&#10;treat molecules as graphs and retrosynthesis/reaction&#10;prediction as a graph-to-sequence translation problem, with&#10;permutation invariance helping the model respect the fact that&#10;atoms and bonds can be represented in multiple equivalent&#10;orders. This matters because it makes reaction prediction more&#10;chemically natural and more scalable than hand-authored&#10;templates. The transition to the 2024 paper, **“Data-&#10;Efficient, Chemistry-Aware Machine Learning Predictions of&#10;Diels–Alder Reaction Outcomes,”** reflects a response to a key&#10;limitation of that earlier paradigm: generic learned&#10;translation models can still be data-hungry and may not&#10;explicitly encode the chemical logic of bond breaking and bond&#10;formation. The 2024 NERF model shifts the emphasis from broad&#10;sequence/graph translation to **chemistry-aware reaction&#10;modeling**, explicitly mimicking bonding changes during&#10;reactions. The concrete advance is a move from “learn the&#10;transformation statistically” to “learn the transformation&#10;while respecting reaction mechanics,” enabling more accurate&#10;and data-efficient predictions for Diels–Alder outcomes. This&#10;is important because it shows that embedding chemical&#10;structure and mechanism into the model can reduce dependence&#10;on very large datasets and improve chemical plausibility.&#10;Paper 1 to Paper 2: The 2024 NERF paper demonstrates that&#10;mechanism-aware representations can improve data efficiency,&#10;but it remains largely focused on a specific reaction class&#10;and does not fully solve the broader problem of making&#10;reliable predictions when experimental data are scarce. The&#10;2025 paper, **“A meta-learning approach for selectivity&#10;prediction in asymmetr..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="968" width="560" height="2076" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3144" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: AI-assisted organic chemistry for&#10;retrosynthesis prediction, reaction outcome prediction, and&#10;multi-step synthesis pathway planning, especially using graph&#10;learning, mechanism-aware representations, and data-efficient&#10;learning.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Current methods do not provide a unified, mechanism-&#10;constrained, data-efficient, uncertainty-aware modeling&#10;framework that can generalize across reaction classes and&#10;jointly support feasible, selective, interpretable multi-step&#10;synthesis planning under scarce, noisy, and biased&#10;experimental data.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- molecular graphs&#10;- target molecules&#10;- precursors and reactants&#10;- reaction products&#10;- retrosynthetic disconnections&#10;- reaction pathways&#10;- chemical mechanisms&#10;- bond-breaking and bond-forming events&#10;- reaction conditions&#10;- catalysts&#10;- selectivity and enantioselectivity&#10;- reaction yield&#10;- reaction feasibility&#10;- literature-derived reaction data&#10;- few-shot reaction tasks&#10;- graph-to-sequence models&#10;- chemistry-aware reaction models&#10;- meta-learning models&#10;- permutation-invariant molecular representations&#10;- uncertainty estimates&#10;- synthetic route practicality&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- molecules are represented as graphs whose atoms and bonds&#10;are transformed by reactions&#10;- permutation-invariant graph encoding makes molecular&#10;predictions independent of atom ordering&#10;- retrosynthesis decomposes a target molecule into feasible&#10;precursor sets&#10;- reaction prediction maps reactants, conditions, and&#10;catalysts to likely products and selectivity&#10;- chemical mechanisms constrain plausible bond changes and&#10;retrosynthetic disconnections&#10;- catalysts and reaction conditions influence feasibility,&#10;yield, and stereochemical outcome&#10;- literature reaction data provide training examples for&#10;learned transformation models&#10;- meta-learning transfers shared reaction features from known&#10;reactions to low-data new reactions&#10;- mechanism-aware representations improve data efficiency and&#10;chemical plausibility&#10;- pathway planning connects individual reaction predictions&#10;into multi-step synthetic routes&#10;- uncertainty and interpretability determine whether&#10;predictions can guide experimental decisions&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- dependence on large, curated, positive-only reaction&#10;datasets&#10;- poor generalization to new reaction classes, catalysts,&#10;substrates, or selectivity regimes&#10;- difficulty predicting stereochemical selectivity and complex&#10;reaction outcomes&#10;- fragmentation of retrosynthesis, outcome prediction, yield&#10;prediction, condition recommendation, and route feasibility&#10;- incomplete mechanistic constraints leading to plausible but&#10;experimentally invalid routes&#10;- lack of calibrated uncertainty for proposed steps and routes&#10;- limited interpretability that chemists can verify and trust&#10;- noisy, biased, and incomplete experimental reporting&#10;- narrow domain-specific performance of data-efficient and&#10;few-shot models&#10;- absence of multi-objective optimization for cost, safety,&#10;sustainability, and scalability&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- generic end-to-end learning versus chemically constrained&#10;mechanistic modeling&#10;- broad coverage across chemistry versus data efficiency in&#10;narrow low-data reaction families&#10;- predictive performance versus transparent causal&#10;interpretability&#10;- single-task reaction prediction versus unified synthesis&#10;planning&#10;- learned statistical correlation versus mechanistic causal&#10;reasoning&#10;- route generation speed versus experimental robustness and&#10;uncertainty calibration&#10;- use of successful literature data versus learning from&#10;missing, negative, or biased outcomes&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- mechanistically constrained and chemically plausible&#10;- data-efficient and adaptable to few examples&#10;- generalizable across reaction classes and catalyst regimes&#10;- capable of predicting feasibility, product distribution,&#10;yield, selectivity, and conditions&#10;- integrated with multi-step retrosynthetic pathway planning&#10;- provides calibrated uncertainty estimates&#10;- offers interpretable, mechanism-level explanations&#10;- robust to noisy, incomplete, and biased reaction data&#10;- optimizes practical multi-objective route criteria such as&#10;cost, safety, step count, and sustainability&#10;- supports decision-making in early-stage reaction and drug&#10;development&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- reaction-center graph neural networks and graph&#10;transformation models&#10;- bond-change and electron-flow encoders&#10;- mechanistic constraint layers or rule-guided decoding&#10;- neuro-symbolic integration of chemical knowledge and learned&#10;models&#10;- meta-learning and few-shot adaptation over reaction tasks&#10;- multi-task learning across retrosynthesis, outcome&#10;prediction, yield, and selectivity&#10;- probabilistic modeling, ensembles, or conformal prediction&#10;for uncertainty quantification&#10;- causal or counterfactual representations of reaction&#10;behavior&#10;- search and planning algorithms for multi-step synthesis&#10;routes&#10;- retrieval-augmented learning from literature reactions&#10;- active learning and closed-loop experimental optimization&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- mechanism-driven graph rewriting and electron-flow modeling&#10;from computational organic chemistry&#10;- transition-state and catalyst descriptors from computational&#10;catalysis&#10;- few-shot adaptation strategies from low-data scientific&#10;discovery domains&#10;- calibrated uncertainty methods from safety-critical planning&#10;and medical decision support&#10;- multi-objective process optimization from chemical&#10;engineering&#10;- closed-loop autonomous laboratory workflows from&#10;experimental chemistry&#10;- causal explanation methods from interpretable scientific&#10;machine learning&#10;- route-search methods from combinatorial planning and&#10;robotics" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3144" width="560" height="2778" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6022" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed to ground the&#10;solution in retrosynthesis, reaction prediction, mechanism-&#10;aware graph learning, and selectivity modeling. Cross-domain&#10;retrieval is also justified because the RSS bottlenecks&#10;require transferable mechanisms for uncertainty calibration,&#10;few-shot scientific learning, multi-objective planning, causal&#10;interpretability, and closed-loop optimization." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6022" width="560" height="294" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6416" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Bayesian Optimization for Chemical Synthesis in the Era&#10;of Artificial Intelligence: Advances and Applications&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: This review highlights recent advances in the&#10;application of Bayesian optimization to chemical synthesis. In&#10;the era of artificial intelligence, Bayesian optimization has&#10;emerged as a powerful machine learning approach that&#10;transforms reaction engineering by enabling efficient...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Bayesian optimization with known experimental and&#10;design constraints for chemistry applications&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Optimization strategies driven by machine learning,&#10;such as Bayesian optimization, are being explored across&#10;experimental sciences as an efficient alternative to&#10;traditional design of experiment. When combined with automated&#10;laboratory hardware and high-performance computing,...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: operations_research&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Optimization and Learning Under Uncertainty: Algorithms&#10;and Theory for Robust Decision-Making&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: This dissertation focuses on developing algorithms&#10;and theoretical foundations for optimization and learning&#10;under uncertainty, aiming to enhance robust decision-making in&#10;the presence of data ambiguity, noisy labels, and uncertain&#10;constraints. The work addresses critical chal...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: operations_research&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Scenario-based optimization for multi-stage stochastic&#10;decision problems&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: control_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: State space models vs. multi-step predictors in&#10;predictive control: Are state space models complicating safe&#10;data-driven designs?&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: This paper contrasts recursive state space models&#10;and direct multi-step predictors for linear predictive&#10;control. We provide a tutorial exposition for both model&#10;structures to solve the following problems: 1. stochastic&#10;optimal control; 2. system identification; 3. stochastic...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: control_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Learning-based Model Predictive Control for Safe&#10;Exploration and Reinforcement Learning&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Reinforcement learning has been successfully used to&#10;solve difficult tasks in complex unknown environments.&#10;However, these methods typically do not provide any safety&#10;guarantees during the learning process. This is particularly&#10;problematic, since reinforcement learning agent a..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fde9d9;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6416" width="620" height="1410" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step6_candidates_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step6_candidates_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step7" value="Step 7 Analogy Extraction&#10;status: completed&#1